WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.637
This query extracts several components from admission timestamps.

00:00:03.637 --> 00:00:05.797
YEAR and MONTH are straightforward.

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:09.917
DOW gives PostgreSQL's numeric day-of-week representation.

00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:12.372
HOUR gives the clock-hour component.

00:00:12.372 --> 00:00:19.087
These values are useful as analytical features, but they do not preserve the full timestamp or create a time

00:00:19.087 --> 00:00:26.247
bucket on their own. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:26.247 --> 00:00:32.542
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:32.542 --> 00:00:34.432
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:34.432 --> 00:00:40.002
In a real setting such as a hospital calculating length of stay and follow-up dates,

00:00:40.002 --> 00:00:47.212
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:49.747
before discussing syntax.

00:00:49.747 --> 00:00:55.519
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
